1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,972
Hello again, hackers. In the last few lessons,

2
00:00:02,972 --> 00:00:06,522
we turned an innocent-looking input into control of

3
00:00:06,522 --> 00:00:09,411
a program. First the bytes escaped a buffer.

4
00:00:09,411 --> 00:00:11,310
Then they crossed the stack,

5
00:00:11,310 --> 00:00:13,539
replaced a saved return address,

6
00:00:13,539 --> 00:00:17,584
and sent the processor somewhere the original programmer

7
00:00:17,584 --> 00:00:19,730
never selected. With shellcode,

8
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:22,042
that destination was our own data.

9
00:00:22,042 --> 00:00:24,684
With ROP, it was a chain of instructions

10
00:00:24,684 --> 00:00:26,830
already inside the executable.

11
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:30,209
Our earlier targets were intentionally generous.

12
00:00:30,209 --> 00:00:31,818
The stack could execute.

13
00:00:31,818 --> 00:00:35,037
The executable lived at predictable addresses.

14
00:00:35,037 --> 00:00:37,933
No secret value guarded the return address.

15
00:00:37,933 --> 00:00:40,025
Linker data remained writable.

16
00:00:40,025 --> 00:00:42,438
That was not a mistake in the lessons;

17
00:00:42,438 --> 00:00:45,898
stripping away defenses let us isolate one exploit

18
00:00:45,898 --> 00:00:47,185
primitive at a time.

19
00:00:47,185 --> 00:00:49,519
But it also gave us a distorted idea

20
00:00:49,519 --> 00:00:52,978
of what a contemporary Linux binary normally looks

21
00:00:52,978 --> 00:00:53,300
like.

22
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:55,675
So today, we reverse perspectives.

23
00:00:55,675 --> 00:00:57,201
We keep the memory bug,

24
00:00:57,201 --> 00:00:59,576
then add the defenses one at a time

25
00:00:59,576 --> 00:01:03,137
and observe exactly what changes—from source code,

26
00:01:03,137 --> 00:01:05,342
to ELF metadata, to disassembly,

27
00:01:05,342 --> 00:01:06,954
to the live process map.

28
00:01:06,954 --> 00:01:08,904
And one historical warning:

29
00:01:08,904 --> 00:01:11,872
these defenses did not all emerge from one

30
00:01:11,872 --> 00:01:14,077
neat sequence of famous papers.

31
00:01:14,077 --> 00:01:17,130
StackGuard has a canonical research paper.

32
00:01:17,130 --> 00:01:22,643
PaX developed executable-space protection and ASLR through implementation

33
00:01:22,643 --> 00:01:25,950
documents. PIE and RELRO grew through compiler,

34
00:01:25,950 --> 00:01:29,173
linker, loader, and distribution engineering.

35
00:01:29,173 --> 00:01:32,480
We will show those primary artifacts honestly.

36
00:01:32,480 --> 00:01:35,005
Before discussing a single instruction,

37
00:01:35,005 --> 00:01:37,011
here is the complete laboratory.

38
00:01:37,011 --> 00:01:39,239
It contains five small source files,

39
00:01:39,239 --> 00:01:41,170
one reproducible build script,

40
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:43,546
one capture script, thirteen binaries,

41
00:01:43,546 --> 00:01:46,443
and the raw outputs used throughout this video.

42
00:01:46,443 --> 00:01:49,414
We are not beginning from a cropped instruction

43
00:01:49,414 --> 00:01:50,454
or a tidy diagram.

44
00:01:50,454 --> 00:01:53,276
We are beginning from the files that produced

45
00:01:53,276 --> 00:01:54,390
every later claim.

46
00:01:54,390 --> 00:01:57,733
This capture was made on sixty-four-bit Linux with

47
00:01:57,733 --> 00:02:00,669
GCC fourteen point two and GNU binutils two

48
00:02:00,669 --> 00:02:04,502
point forty-four. Kernel address randomization is set to

49
00:02:04,502 --> 00:02:06,785
mode two. We do not disable ASLR for

50
00:02:06,785 --> 00:02:10,536
convenient screenshots, and we do not invent addresses

51
00:02:10,536 --> 00:02:12,819
that remain stable across reruns.

52
00:02:12,819 --> 00:02:14,939
When this lab is captured again,

53
00:02:14,939 --> 00:02:18,201
several pointers and process IDs should change.

54
00:02:18,201 --> 00:02:19,832
That change is evidence,

55
00:02:19,832 --> 00:02:21,300
not an inconvenience.

56
00:02:21,300 --> 00:02:23,723
This is the complete vulnerable program.

57
00:02:23,723 --> 00:02:26,076
There is no hidden helper and no omitted

58
00:02:26,076 --> 00:02:29,283
cleanup path. `greet` reserves thirty-two bytes for `name`,

59
00:02:29,283 --> 00:02:32,277
then passes that address to `read` while requesting

60
00:02:32,277 --> 00:02:34,914
as many as one hundred and twenty-eight bytes.

61
00:02:34,914 --> 00:02:37,979
`main` disables output buffering only so the runtime

62
00:02:37,979 --> 00:02:40,402
evidence appears in a predictable order.

63
00:02:40,402 --> 00:02:43,610
Every protected variant begins with this same source.

64
00:02:43,610 --> 00:02:45,688
When we build the variants,

65
00:02:45,688 --> 00:02:48,899
GCC warns us before the program ever runs.

66
00:02:48,899 --> 00:02:51,450
The warning is unusually direct:

67
00:02:51,450 --> 00:02:55,322
`read` may write one hundred and twenty-eight bytes

68
00:02:55,322 --> 00:02:57,872
into an object of size thirty-two.

69
00:02:57,872 --> 00:02:59,572
That warning is not NX,

70
00:02:59,572 --> 00:03:00,895
a canary, ASLR, PIE,

71
00:03:00,895 --> 00:03:03,539
or RELRO. It identifies the defect.

72
00:03:03,539 --> 00:03:07,601
The mitigations we study later only constrain what

73
00:03:07,601 --> 00:03:11,379
can happen after the defective write is allowed

74
00:03:11,379 --> 00:03:12,040
to exist.

75
00:03:12,040 --> 00:03:14,174
The build produces real ELF files,

76
00:03:14,174 --> 00:03:16,385
with distinct build IDs and hashes.

77
00:03:16,385 --> 00:03:20,119
`file` identifies which are ordinary executables and which

78
00:03:20,119 --> 00:03:24,921
are position-independent executables. We record those identities because

79
00:03:24,921 --> 00:03:27,894
later screenshots must point back to the exact

80
00:03:27,894 --> 00:03:29,799
artifact that generated them.

81
00:03:29,799 --> 00:03:32,314
A clean diagram is useful only after the

82
00:03:32,314 --> 00:03:33,915
evidence chain is intact.

83
00:03:33,915 --> 00:03:35,899
Before drawing a stack frame,

84
00:03:35,899 --> 00:03:38,876
we ask the binary what GCC actually emitted.

85
00:03:38,876 --> 00:03:42,597
`objdump` interleaves the full source with the machine

86
00:03:42,597 --> 00:03:46,400
instructions for `greet`. The function starts at address

87
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:48,468
`0x401156`, reserves `0x30` bytes,

88
00:03:48,468 --> 00:03:51,196
calculates the buffer as `rbp minus 0x30`,

89
00:03:51,196 --> 00:03:55,579
loads `0x80`—one hundred and twenty-eight—into the third argument

90
00:03:55,579 --> 00:03:58,803
register, calls `read`, then reaches `leave` and `ret`

91
00:03:58,803 --> 00:04:00,705
without an integrity check.

92
00:04:00,705 --> 00:04:02,849
Now the diagram has a provenance.

93
00:04:02,849 --> 00:04:05,391
`sub rsp,0x30` gives the local frame area.

94
00:04:05,391 --> 00:04:08,012
`rbp minus 0x30` locates the first byte of

95
00:04:08,012 --> 00:04:11,189
`name`. The saved frame pointer and return address

96
00:04:11,189 --> 00:04:13,016
sit above the current frame.

97
00:04:13,016 --> 00:04:16,034
This is still a reconstruction—we did not dump

98
00:04:16,034 --> 00:04:19,449
every stack byte—but its distances are derived from

99
00:04:19,449 --> 00:04:21,832
the instructions we just inspected,

100
00:04:21,832 --> 00:04:24,135
not from a generic textbook layout.

101
00:04:24,135 --> 00:04:26,244
To isolate execute permission,

102
00:04:26,244 --> 00:04:28,434
we use a second complete program.

103
00:04:28,434 --> 00:04:31,192
Its local array contains six x86-64 bytes:

104
00:04:31,192 --> 00:04:32,084
`mov eax, 42; ret`.

105
00:04:32,084 --> 00:04:34,680
The program prints the array’s address,

106
00:04:34,680 --> 00:04:37,438
casts that address to a function pointer,

107
00:04:37,438 --> 00:04:40,358
and calls it. The two binaries are identical

108
00:04:40,358 --> 00:04:43,603
except for the linker’s stack-execution request.

109
00:04:43,603 --> 00:04:46,198
This is safer and clearer than asking a

110
00:04:46,198 --> 00:04:49,281
full shell payload to prove several things at

111
00:04:49,281 --> 00:04:49,605
once.

112
00:04:49,605 --> 00:04:52,575
First we inspect the ELF program headers,

113
00:04:52,575 --> 00:04:55,108
not a hand-written permission card.

114
00:04:55,108 --> 00:04:57,292
In the executable-stack build,

115
00:04:57,292 --> 00:04:59,389
the `GNU_STACK` row ends in `RWE`:

116
00:04:59,389 --> 00:05:02,446
read, write, execute. In the protected build,

117
00:05:02,446 --> 00:05:04,019
the same row ends in `RW`:

118
00:05:04,019 --> 00:05:06,552
read and write, with execute absent.

119
00:05:06,552 --> 00:05:10,308
The close-up only appears after the complete header

120
00:05:10,308 --> 00:05:13,715
table has established where that row came from.

121
00:05:13,715 --> 00:05:15,336
Now we run both artifacts.

122
00:05:15,336 --> 00:05:17,034
With the executable stack,

123
00:05:17,034 --> 00:05:19,350
the program prints its stack buffer,

124
00:05:19,350 --> 00:05:22,669
calls those bytes, and returns forty-two with status

125
00:05:22,669 --> 00:05:25,217
zero. With NX, the same call is terminated

126
00:05:25,217 --> 00:05:28,150
by signal eleven and the shell reports status

127
00:05:28,150 --> 00:05:29,925
one hundred and thirty-nine.

128
00:05:29,925 --> 00:05:32,396
The bytes were accepted as data in both

129
00:05:32,396 --> 00:05:35,406
cases. The difference appears only when the CPU

130
00:05:35,406 --> 00:05:38,185
tries to fetch instructions from that page.

131
00:05:38,185 --> 00:05:41,085
The wider idea is executable-space protection.

132
00:05:41,085 --> 00:05:44,803
PaX developed PAGEEXEC and related mechanisms for systems

133
00:05:44,803 --> 00:05:48,595
where convenient hardware support was not always available,

134
00:05:48,595 --> 00:05:51,198
and its documents describe making stacks,

135
00:05:51,198 --> 00:05:55,734
heaps, and anonymous mappings non-executable while reserving execution

136
00:05:55,734 --> 00:05:57,667
for mappings that contain code.

137
00:05:57,667 --> 00:06:00,344
NX is not a detector looking for suspicious

138
00:06:00,344 --> 00:06:03,095
byte patterns. It is permission enforcement.

139
00:06:03,095 --> 00:06:06,404
The write into the array succeeds because the

140
00:06:06,404 --> 00:06:10,062
stack is writable. The indirect call also succeeds

141
00:06:10,062 --> 00:06:13,371
in changing RIP because NX is not control-flow

142
00:06:13,371 --> 00:06:16,506
integrity. Failure occurs at the next stage:

143
00:06:16,506 --> 00:06:21,383
instruction fetch. The translation and permission machinery sees

144
00:06:21,383 --> 00:06:24,866
an address in a page without execute permission,

145
00:06:24,866 --> 00:06:27,740
so no instruction from `b8 2a...` reaches the

146
00:06:27,740 --> 00:06:29,656
decoder. The data survives;

147
00:06:29,656 --> 00:06:31,485
execution does not begin.

148
00:06:31,485 --> 00:06:33,808
This is why ROP was the next logical

149
00:06:33,808 --> 00:06:38,694
technique. The corrupted stack supplies data—addresses and values—while

150
00:06:38,694 --> 00:06:42,459
instruction fetches stay inside executable code pages.

151
00:06:42,459 --> 00:06:45,983
NX removed the assumption that writable bytes could

152
00:06:45,983 --> 00:06:48,787
also be instructions. It did not remove the

153
00:06:48,787 --> 00:06:51,670
overflow, stop `ret`, or erase the program’s own

154
00:06:51,670 --> 00:06:54,394
executable code. One attack route closed;

155
00:06:54,394 --> 00:06:55,595
another remained.

156
00:06:55,595 --> 00:06:58,386
To see what stack protection changes,

157
00:06:58,386 --> 00:07:01,447
we compare the complete `greet` functions.

158
00:07:01,447 --> 00:07:05,769
The unprotected version reserves `0x30` bytes and returns

159
00:07:05,769 --> 00:07:09,280
directly. The protected version reserves `0x40`,

160
00:07:09,280 --> 00:07:11,171
loads a value from `fs:0x28`,

161
00:07:11,171 --> 00:07:13,242
stores it at `rbp minus eight`,

162
00:07:13,242 --> 00:07:15,853
performs the same vulnerable `read`,

163
00:07:15,853 --> 00:07:19,635
then reloads and compares that saved value before

164
00:07:19,635 --> 00:07:20,625
`leave` and `ret`.

165
00:07:20,625 --> 00:07:24,402
The disassembly tells us where the compiler reads

166
00:07:24,402 --> 00:07:25,931
and stores the guard,

167
00:07:25,931 --> 00:07:28,450
but we can also measure the values.

168
00:07:28,450 --> 00:07:33,666
This architecture-specific probe reads the x86-64 thread-local guard

169
00:07:33,666 --> 00:07:37,084
from `fs:0x28` and the compiler’s frame copy from

170
00:07:37,084 --> 00:07:39,512
`rbp minus eight`, then prints both.

171
00:07:39,512 --> 00:07:42,120
It is instrumentation for this lab,

172
00:07:42,120 --> 00:07:44,459
not portable application code,

173
00:07:44,459 --> 00:07:48,146
and its complete source remains on screen before

174
00:07:48,146 --> 00:07:49,855
we inspect the numbers.

175
00:07:49,855 --> 00:07:52,707
Across three executions, the guard changes.

176
00:07:52,707 --> 00:07:54,450
In each execution, however,

177
00:07:54,450 --> 00:07:57,382
the thread-local guard and the frame copy are

178
00:07:57,382 --> 00:08:00,076
identical. The first run prints `cc6f...2200`;

179
00:08:00,076 --> 00:08:01,898
the second prints `8f19...7a00`;

180
00:08:01,898 --> 00:08:03,641
the third prints `5b33...3800`.

181
00:08:03,641 --> 00:08:07,207
We are not choosing aesthetically convenient values.

182
00:08:07,207 --> 00:08:09,980
These are the values recorded by the probe

183
00:08:09,980 --> 00:08:11,010
on this capture.

184
00:08:11,010 --> 00:08:14,344
In 1998, Crispin Cowan and his coauthors presented

185
00:08:14,344 --> 00:08:17,837
StackGuard. Their paper places a canary word between

186
00:08:17,837 --> 00:08:20,060
local data and the return address,

187
00:08:20,060 --> 00:08:23,076
then verifies it before the function returns.

188
00:08:23,076 --> 00:08:26,252
Its Figure Two is not merely decorative history;

189
00:08:26,252 --> 00:08:29,586
it gives us a primary-source stack relationship to

190
00:08:29,586 --> 00:08:32,920
compare with the compiler output we just measured.

191
00:08:32,920 --> 00:08:36,899
At function entry, the protected binary reserves sixty-four

192
00:08:36,899 --> 00:08:41,118
bytes. Instruction `0x40116e` copies the thread-local guard into

193
00:08:41,118 --> 00:08:44,301
`rax`. Instruction `0x401177` stores it at `rbp minus

194
00:08:44,301 --> 00:08:47,007
eight`. The buffer begins at `rbp minus 0x30`.

195
00:08:47,007 --> 00:08:49,634
That puts the canary above the buffer in

196
00:08:49,634 --> 00:08:51,783
the path toward saved frame data.

197
00:08:51,783 --> 00:08:53,932
The diagram is a reconstruction,

198
00:08:53,932 --> 00:08:57,195
but every distance shown comes from the selected

199
00:08:57,195 --> 00:08:58,150
instructions.

200
00:08:58,150 --> 00:09:00,407
The canary does not stop the write.

201
00:09:00,407 --> 00:09:03,954
The first thirty-two bytes occupy the declared array.

202
00:09:03,954 --> 00:09:07,501
More bytes continue through adjacent frame storage.

203
00:09:07,501 --> 00:09:10,886
Eventually the stream overwrites the saved guard,

204
00:09:10,886 --> 00:09:13,708
then can continue toward saved `rbp` and the

205
00:09:13,708 --> 00:09:16,932
return address. The defensive action is delayed.

206
00:09:16,932 --> 00:09:20,398
Stack protection records that corruption now so it

207
00:09:20,398 --> 00:09:23,300
can react before control is returned later.

208
00:09:23,300 --> 00:09:26,081
The epilogue reloads the frame copy at `0x4011b8`,

209
00:09:26,081 --> 00:09:29,210
subtracts the current thread-local guard at `0x4011bc`,

210
00:09:29,210 --> 00:09:31,435
and jumps over the failure call only if

211
00:09:31,435 --> 00:09:32,478
the result is zero.

212
00:09:32,478 --> 00:09:33,660
If the values differ,

213
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:37,345
`0x4011c7` calls `__stack_chk_fail`. Only after the equality path

214
00:09:37,345 --> 00:09:39,779
reaches `0x4011cc` do `leave` and `ret` execute.

215
00:09:39,779 --> 00:09:42,630
The corrupted return address can exist in memory

216
00:09:42,630 --> 00:09:44,090
and still never reach RIP.

217
00:09:44,090 --> 00:09:45,556
With ninety-six `A` bytes,

218
00:09:45,556 --> 00:09:48,488
the plain build prints that it read ninety-six

219
00:09:48,488 --> 00:09:51,343
bytes, then dies by signal eleven with status

220
00:09:51,343 --> 00:09:53,118
one hundred and thirty-nine.

221
00:09:53,118 --> 00:09:56,205
The canary build reads the same ninety-six bytes,

222
00:09:56,205 --> 00:09:58,288
prints `stack smashing detected`,

223
00:09:58,288 --> 00:10:01,221
then terminates by signal six with status one

224
00:10:01,221 --> 00:10:05,156
hundred and thirty-four. One crashes after corrupted control

225
00:10:05,156 --> 00:10:08,706
state is consumed. The other detects corruption before

226
00:10:08,706 --> 00:10:09,400
returning.

227
00:10:09,400 --> 00:10:12,154
A stack canary is therefore a tamper check

228
00:10:12,154 --> 00:10:14,356
for particular protected frames,

229
00:10:14,356 --> 00:10:16,795
not a universal memory-safety system.

230
00:10:16,795 --> 00:10:18,290
It does not repair `read`,

231
00:10:18,290 --> 00:10:20,021
protect every heap object,

232
00:10:20,021 --> 00:10:22,853
or make all pointer corruption impossible.

233
00:10:22,853 --> 00:10:25,764
And if another bug reveals the current guard,

234
00:10:25,764 --> 00:10:29,462
an attacker may reproduce that value while overwriting

235
00:10:29,462 --> 00:10:32,215
data around it. The next defenses also rely

236
00:10:32,215 --> 00:10:35,992
on hidden values—and that makes disclosure our recurring

237
00:10:35,992 --> 00:10:36,385
enemy.

238
00:10:36,385 --> 00:10:38,891
To observe address randomization,

239
00:10:38,891 --> 00:10:41,225
we use another complete program.

240
00:10:41,225 --> 00:10:42,953
It prints its process ID,

241
00:10:42,953 --> 00:10:44,336
the address of `main`,

242
00:10:44,336 --> 00:10:46,151
one local stack variable,

243
00:10:46,151 --> 00:10:47,533
a reference to `puts`,

244
00:10:47,533 --> 00:10:51,336
and the actual libc implementation found with `dlsym`.

245
00:10:51,336 --> 00:10:52,891
If launched with `--hold`,

246
00:10:52,891 --> 00:10:55,484
it waits so we can inspect `/proc` while

247
00:10:55,484 --> 00:10:57,904
that exact process is still alive.

248
00:10:57,904 --> 00:11:01,015
The kernel reports randomization mode two.

249
00:11:01,015 --> 00:11:03,948
Across four runs of the non-PIE binary,

250
00:11:03,948 --> 00:11:07,733
`main` remains `0x401186`, and its PLT reference for

251
00:11:07,733 --> 00:11:12,085
`puts` remains `0x401030`. The local stack pointer changes

252
00:11:12,085 --> 00:11:15,870
every time. The actual libc `puts` address changes

253
00:11:15,870 --> 00:11:19,843
every time. This is the first important separation:

254
00:11:19,843 --> 00:11:23,155
ASLR is active, but an ordinary non-PIE main

255
00:11:23,155 --> 00:11:26,845
executable can still provide fixed addresses.

256
00:11:26,845 --> 00:11:29,104
This is the actual map for that held

257
00:11:29,104 --> 00:11:32,065
process. The executable begins at `0x00400000`.

258
00:11:32,065 --> 00:11:34,636
Its executable page is the next mapping.

259
00:11:34,636 --> 00:11:37,597
Libc’s file mapping begins at `0x7f704aa1e000`,

260
00:11:37,597 --> 00:11:40,869
and the user stack occupies the high-address range

261
00:11:40,869 --> 00:11:44,141
beginning at `0x7ffe4a34d000`. We show the whole map

262
00:11:44,141 --> 00:11:47,336
first because a selected row without its process

263
00:11:47,336 --> 00:11:49,595
and file context is easy to misread.

264
00:11:49,595 --> 00:11:52,823
The PaX ASLR design states the central objective

265
00:11:52,823 --> 00:11:57,311
plainly: introduce randomness into addresses so attacks requiring

266
00:11:57,311 --> 00:11:59,831
advance knowledge become unreliable.

267
00:11:59,831 --> 00:12:02,193
It also states the central weakness:

268
00:12:02,193 --> 00:12:05,500
if the process can disclose its randomized layout,

269
00:12:05,500 --> 00:12:07,626
guessing is no longer necessary.

270
00:12:07,626 --> 00:12:10,697
The protection is not that the addresses cease

271
00:12:10,697 --> 00:12:13,295
to exist. It is that their current values

272
00:12:13,295 --> 00:12:15,185
become secret process state.

273
00:12:15,185 --> 00:12:18,517
ASLR does not normally throw every function into

274
00:12:18,517 --> 00:12:20,386
an independent random slot.

275
00:12:20,386 --> 00:12:23,474
It randomizes the base of mappings or regions.

276
00:12:23,474 --> 00:12:25,425
In one particular libc build,

277
00:12:25,425 --> 00:12:28,025
`puts` remains at a fixed offset from the

278
00:12:28,025 --> 00:12:30,544
library’s base. When the library moves,

279
00:12:30,544 --> 00:12:32,657
the base and `puts` move together.

280
00:12:32,657 --> 00:12:36,639
That preserved internal distance is precisely what later

281
00:12:36,639 --> 00:12:39,727
allows one leaked pointer to reveal the whole

282
00:12:39,727 --> 00:12:40,215
region.

283
00:12:40,215 --> 00:12:41,815
For PID fifteen twenty-one,

284
00:12:41,815 --> 00:12:45,014
the captured libc mapping begins at `0x7f704aa1e000`,

285
00:12:45,014 --> 00:12:47,705
while `dlsym` reports `puts` at `0x7f704aa9e5a0`.

286
00:12:47,705 --> 00:12:50,831
`nm` independently reports the `puts` symbol offset as

287
00:12:50,831 --> 00:12:52,722
`0x805a0` in this exact libc file.

288
00:12:52,722 --> 00:12:55,485
Adding the captured base and that file offset

289
00:12:55,485 --> 00:12:57,957
reproduces the runtime address exactly.

290
00:12:57,957 --> 00:13:00,575
These numbers belong to this build and this

291
00:13:00,575 --> 00:13:03,120
run; the relationship is the reusable part.

292
00:13:03,120 --> 00:13:06,655
Address randomization was also studied as a broader

293
00:13:06,655 --> 00:13:08,664
compiler and loader strategy.

294
00:13:08,664 --> 00:13:13,163
Bhatkar, DuVarney, and Sekar described address obfuscation across

295
00:13:13,163 --> 00:13:16,778
code and data. Shacham and colleagues later evaluated

296
00:13:16,778 --> 00:13:19,912
the practical limits of ASLR in a thirty-two-bit

297
00:13:19,912 --> 00:13:24,411
setting, where restricted entropy and repeated attempts mattered

298
00:13:24,411 --> 00:13:27,785
greatly. The correct lesson is neither ‘ASLR solves

299
00:13:27,785 --> 00:13:31,481
exploitation’ nor ‘ASLR is useless.’ Its strength depends

300
00:13:31,481 --> 00:13:33,409
on entropy, restart behavior,

301
00:13:33,409 --> 00:13:37,105
architecture, and especially information disclosure.

302
00:13:37,105 --> 00:13:40,187
When a program reveals a pointer into libc,

303
00:13:40,187 --> 00:13:43,270
the attacker no longer needs to guess that

304
00:13:43,270 --> 00:13:46,352
run’s base. Subtract the known offset of the

305
00:13:46,352 --> 00:13:50,843
revealed object, and the randomized base becomes measurable.

306
00:13:50,843 --> 00:13:53,045
ASLR has not been switched off.

307
00:13:53,045 --> 00:13:55,599
Its hidden state has been observed.

308
00:13:55,599 --> 00:13:58,769
That is the ASLR part of ret2libc—but before

309
00:13:58,769 --> 00:14:01,764
we can use the main executable to perform

310
00:14:01,764 --> 00:14:04,229
such a leak, we must ask whether the

311
00:14:04,229 --> 00:14:06,255
executable itself is fixed.

312
00:14:06,255 --> 00:14:08,117
PIE is visible before runtime.

313
00:14:08,117 --> 00:14:11,693
`readelf` identifies `address-nopie` as type `EXEC` with entry

314
00:14:11,693 --> 00:14:14,300
point `0x4010a0`; `nm` places `main` at `0x401186`.

315
00:14:14,300 --> 00:14:15,790
The PIE build is type `DYN`,

316
00:14:15,790 --> 00:14:18,918
specifically a position-independent executable,

317
00:14:18,918 --> 00:14:20,408
with entry point `0x10a0`;

318
00:14:20,408 --> 00:14:22,419
`nm` records `main` as offset `0x1189`.

319
00:14:22,419 --> 00:14:25,473
We first inspect the complete headers and symbol

320
00:14:25,473 --> 00:14:27,410
tables, then extract those rows.

321
00:14:27,410 --> 00:14:30,897
PIE and ASLR are related but not synonymous.

322
00:14:30,897 --> 00:14:34,481
PIE is the property that lets the executable

323
00:14:34,481 --> 00:14:36,708
operate at different bases.

324
00:14:36,708 --> 00:14:40,002
ASLR is the runtime policy that selects a

325
00:14:40,002 --> 00:14:42,617
base for a particular execution.

326
00:14:42,617 --> 00:14:46,975
A relocatable executable can be placed somewhere new.

327
00:14:46,975 --> 00:14:51,625
A fixed-address executable still expects its linked home.

328
00:14:51,625 --> 00:14:55,499
Only the combination gives us a randomized main

329
00:14:55,499 --> 00:14:56,080
binary.

330
00:14:56,080 --> 00:14:57,470
In the four PIE executions,

331
00:14:57,470 --> 00:15:00,377
`main` changes from `0x55949c2c2189` to three other bases.

332
00:15:00,377 --> 00:15:02,715
The stack changes, and libc changes as before.

333
00:15:02,715 --> 00:15:05,305
Here the printed `puts` reference also resolves to

334
00:15:05,305 --> 00:15:07,264
the randomized libc implementation.

335
00:15:07,264 --> 00:15:09,855
The important result is that the executable’s own

336
00:15:09,855 --> 00:15:12,003
code address is no longer a stable island

337
00:15:12,003 --> 00:15:12,635
across runs.

338
00:15:12,635 --> 00:15:14,244
For PID fifteen twenty-two,

339
00:15:14,244 --> 00:15:16,949
`/proc` shows the PIE file mapping beginning at

340
00:15:16,949 --> 00:15:20,313
`0x55bce0674000`. The binary’s symbol table gives `main` an

341
00:15:20,313 --> 00:15:21,848
offset of `0x1189`. Add them,

342
00:15:21,848 --> 00:15:24,919
and we obtain the printed runtime address exactly:

343
00:15:24,919 --> 00:15:29,672
`0x55bce0675189`. PIE therefore changes absolute addresses while preserving

344
00:15:29,672 --> 00:15:31,281
offsets within that build,

345
00:15:31,281 --> 00:15:34,425
just as ASLR-preserved offsets existed inside libc.

346
00:15:34,425 --> 00:15:37,966
Without PIE, fixed addresses in the main binary

347
00:15:37,966 --> 00:15:39,600
can provide a PLT call,

348
00:15:39,600 --> 00:15:41,869
a GOT location, a re-entry point,

349
00:15:41,869 --> 00:15:44,593
or a ROP gadget even while libc moves.

350
00:15:44,593 --> 00:15:48,043
With PIE, those objects move together with the

351
00:15:48,043 --> 00:15:51,946
executable. An exploit often needs a binary pointer

352
00:15:51,946 --> 00:15:54,398
leak before using them reliably.

353
00:15:54,398 --> 00:15:56,940
But PIE does not erase the objects,

354
00:15:56,940 --> 00:16:00,480
change their internal offsets on every launch,

355
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:03,658
fix the overflow, or conceal a pointer that

356
00:16:03,658 --> 00:16:05,655
the program itself prints.

357
00:16:05,655 --> 00:16:08,146
To observe dynamic binding order,

358
00:16:08,146 --> 00:16:10,549
we use a fourth complete program.

359
00:16:10,549 --> 00:16:14,464
It writes explicit markers to standard error before

360
00:16:14,464 --> 00:16:16,777
and after the first call to `puts`,

361
00:16:16,777 --> 00:16:20,070
then sleeps so its mappings can be inspected.

362
00:16:20,070 --> 00:16:23,718
Three non-PIE binaries are built from this source:

363
00:16:23,718 --> 00:16:25,675
no RELRO with lazy binding,

364
00:16:25,675 --> 00:16:28,078
partial RELRO with lazy binding,

365
00:16:28,078 --> 00:16:30,925
and full RELRO with immediate binding.

366
00:16:30,925 --> 00:16:34,015
RELRO cannot be understood from one green label.

367
00:16:34,015 --> 00:16:36,719
For each binary, we record program headers,

368
00:16:36,719 --> 00:16:39,345
the dynamic section, relocation records,

369
00:16:39,345 --> 00:16:42,281
and section headers. No RELRO has no `GNU_RELRO`

370
00:16:42,281 --> 00:16:45,680
segment. Partial RELRO has one beginning at `0x403df8`,

371
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:48,152
but no `BIND_NOW`. Full RELRO has a larger

372
00:16:48,152 --> 00:16:50,701
protected range beginning at `0x403dc0`,

373
00:16:50,701 --> 00:16:54,023
and its dynamic section contains both `BIND_NOW` and

374
00:16:54,023 --> 00:16:54,795
the `NOW` flag.

375
00:16:54,795 --> 00:16:57,666
Next we connect the file’s relocation address to

376
00:16:57,666 --> 00:16:59,963
the live process page that contains it.

377
00:16:59,963 --> 00:17:01,255
In the no-RELRO binary,

378
00:17:01,255 --> 00:17:03,551
`puts` has relocation address `0x4033a0`,

379
00:17:03,551 --> 00:17:05,130
inside a writable mapping.

380
00:17:05,130 --> 00:17:07,427
With partial RELRO, `puts` is at `0x404000`,

381
00:17:07,427 --> 00:17:09,365
again inside a writable mapping.

382
00:17:09,365 --> 00:17:11,446
With full RELRO, `puts` is at `0x403fd8`,

383
00:17:11,446 --> 00:17:14,030
and the containing page is mapped read-only.

384
00:17:14,030 --> 00:17:16,242
To see *when* symbols are resolved,

385
00:17:16,242 --> 00:17:19,682
we ask the dynamic loader for binding diagnostics.

386
00:17:19,682 --> 00:17:22,549
The raw output is dense—more than a hundred

387
00:17:22,549 --> 00:17:26,808
lines—because the loader reports bindings for the executable,

388
00:17:26,808 --> 00:17:28,610
libc, and the loader itself.

389
00:17:28,610 --> 00:17:30,985
We show that complete stream first.

390
00:17:30,985 --> 00:17:33,606
Only then do we select the lines around

391
00:17:33,606 --> 00:17:36,555
`transferring control`, our program markers,

392
00:17:36,555 --> 00:17:38,357
and the bindings for `write`,

393
00:17:38,357 --> 00:17:39,340
`puts`, and `sleep`.

394
00:17:39,340 --> 00:17:40,743
In the partial build,

395
00:17:40,743 --> 00:17:44,210
the loader transfers control to the program first.

396
00:17:44,210 --> 00:17:46,852
`write` is bound as the first marker runs.

397
00:17:46,852 --> 00:17:49,823
The program announces that it is before its

398
00:17:49,823 --> 00:17:52,630
first `puts`. Only then does the loader bind

399
00:17:52,630 --> 00:17:54,859
`puts`. `sleep` is bound later as well.

400
00:17:54,859 --> 00:17:59,151
Lazy binding postpones these writes until the corresponding

401
00:17:59,151 --> 00:18:00,307
call path is used,

402
00:18:00,307 --> 00:18:03,856
which is why the procedure-linkage slot must remain

403
00:18:03,856 --> 00:18:06,250
writable during normal execution.

404
00:18:06,250 --> 00:18:07,325
In the full build,

405
00:18:07,325 --> 00:18:09,935
the order reverses. The loader binds `puts`,

406
00:18:09,935 --> 00:18:12,929
`write`, and `sleep` before the line that transfers

407
00:18:12,929 --> 00:18:14,618
control to the executable.

408
00:18:14,618 --> 00:18:17,535
Only afterward do our `entered main` and `before

409
00:18:17,535 --> 00:18:19,224
first puts` markers appear.

410
00:18:19,224 --> 00:18:23,676
Because the required procedure-linkage writes are completed during

411
00:18:23,676 --> 00:18:26,823
startup, the page containing `puts` can be read-only

412
00:18:26,823 --> 00:18:29,280
by the time ordinary program code runs.

413
00:18:29,280 --> 00:18:33,415
Drepper’s engineering report explains the layout problem behind

414
00:18:33,415 --> 00:18:36,498
RELRO. Some ELF structures must be writable while

415
00:18:36,498 --> 00:18:40,182
relocations are performed, but should not remain writable

416
00:18:40,182 --> 00:18:43,640
afterward. The report’s own diagrams show protected ELF

417
00:18:43,640 --> 00:18:46,347
data reordered next to read-only data so the

418
00:18:46,347 --> 00:18:48,828
loader can cover it with a read-only page

419
00:18:48,828 --> 00:18:52,361
range after relocation. Full RELRO combines that layout

420
00:18:52,361 --> 00:18:53,865
with immediate binding.

421
00:18:53,865 --> 00:18:56,887
The conceptual calling path is now safe to

422
00:18:56,887 --> 00:19:00,427
animate because we have already located its real

423
00:19:00,427 --> 00:19:03,276
relocation entries and binding events.

424
00:19:03,276 --> 00:19:05,261
A call reaches the PLT route.

425
00:19:05,261 --> 00:19:08,197
The route consults the relevant GOT slot.

426
00:19:08,197 --> 00:19:11,909
Before resolution, that route can enter the dynamic

427
00:19:11,909 --> 00:19:15,794
resolver. After resolution, the slot contains the libc

428
00:19:15,794 --> 00:19:20,111
destination. Partial RELRO permits later loader writes for

429
00:19:20,111 --> 00:19:22,960
lazy binding. Full RELRO resolves first,

430
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:25,205
then removes write permission.

431
00:19:25,205 --> 00:19:29,006
Full RELRO blocks writes to the protected relocation

432
00:19:29,006 --> 00:19:31,878
page. It does not make the page unreadable.

433
00:19:31,878 --> 00:19:33,905
The mapping we measured is `r--p`,

434
00:19:33,905 --> 00:19:37,875
not inaccessible. A disclosure primitive may still read

435
00:19:37,875 --> 00:19:39,734
the resolved `puts` pointer,

436
00:19:39,734 --> 00:19:43,366
and that pointer can still reveal libc’s randomized

437
00:19:43,366 --> 00:19:46,322
base. RELRO closes the GOT-overwrite route.

438
00:19:46,322 --> 00:19:48,265
It does not close code reuse,

439
00:19:48,265 --> 00:19:52,995
information leakage, or corruption of unrelated writable objects.

440
00:19:52,995 --> 00:19:56,107
Only now do we compress the evidence into

441
00:19:56,107 --> 00:19:59,859
a summary. NX comes from stack execution metadata

442
00:19:59,859 --> 00:20:04,710
and page enforcement. The canary appears as compiler-generated

443
00:20:04,710 --> 00:20:07,547
prologue and epilogue instructions.

444
00:20:07,547 --> 00:20:10,842
ASLR appears in changing runtime mappings.

445
00:20:10,842 --> 00:20:13,587
PIE changes the ELF type and lets the

446
00:20:13,587 --> 00:20:16,516
main binary receive a randomized base.

447
00:20:16,516 --> 00:20:18,530
RELRO combines ELF layout,

448
00:20:18,530 --> 00:20:22,465
binding policy, and loader-enforced read-only pages.

449
00:20:22,465 --> 00:20:25,292
The source-level defect never changed:

450
00:20:25,292 --> 00:20:27,324
thirty-two bytes of storage,

451
00:20:27,324 --> 00:20:30,681
one hundred and twenty-eight bytes requested.

452
00:20:30,681 --> 00:20:33,420
What changed was the point at which an

453
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:38,103
exploit’s assumptions failed. NX waits until instruction fetch.

454
00:20:38,103 --> 00:20:40,223
The canary reacts before `ret`.

455
00:20:40,223 --> 00:20:43,669
ASLR invalidates yesterday’s runtime pointer.

456
00:20:43,669 --> 00:20:46,673
PIE removes fixed main-binary addresses.

457
00:20:46,673 --> 00:20:49,942
Full RELRO rejects a write to the relocation

458
00:20:49,942 --> 00:20:50,295
page.

459
00:20:50,295 --> 00:20:52,750
No single layer is the final answer.

460
00:20:52,750 --> 00:20:54,359
ROP can route around NX.

461
00:20:54,359 --> 00:20:57,492
A pointer disclosure can reveal an ASLR base.

462
00:20:57,492 --> 00:21:00,624
A leaked canary can preserve the stack check.

463
00:21:00,624 --> 00:21:03,249
A leaked code pointer can reveal a PIE

464
00:21:03,249 --> 00:21:06,213
base. Full RELRO can force an attacker away

465
00:21:06,213 --> 00:21:10,615
from GOT overwrites without preventing every code-reuse path.

466
00:21:10,615 --> 00:21:13,833
Defense in depth works by making a successful

467
00:21:13,833 --> 00:21:18,151
chain require several independent capabilities in the same

468
00:21:18,151 --> 00:21:19,590
vulnerable process.

469
00:21:19,590 --> 00:21:22,527
Return once more to the compiler warning.

470
00:21:22,527 --> 00:21:26,674
It identified the actual engineering failure before any

471
00:21:26,674 --> 00:21:30,562
mitigation activated. NX did not reduce the requested

472
00:21:30,562 --> 00:21:33,931
length. The canary did not make the destination

473
00:21:33,931 --> 00:21:36,782
larger. ASLR and PIE did not validate the

474
00:21:36,782 --> 00:21:39,460
pointer. RELRO did not constrain `read`.

475
00:21:39,460 --> 00:21:42,656
A corrected bound, a memory-safe abstraction,

476
00:21:42,656 --> 00:21:45,939
or a design that cannot express this mismatch

477
00:21:45,939 --> 00:21:47,753
addresses the bug itself.

478
00:21:47,753 --> 00:21:51,900
The protections reduce exploitability when bugs remain.

479
00:21:51,900 --> 00:21:55,134
And now ret2libc stops looking like a bag

480
00:21:55,134 --> 00:21:58,654
of arbitrary tricks. NX explains why we reuse

481
00:21:58,654 --> 00:22:02,649
executable code instead of running bytes from the

482
00:22:02,649 --> 00:22:05,979
stack. ASLR explains why libc’s base must be

483
00:22:05,979 --> 00:22:09,593
learned. PIE explains why even our PLT entries,

484
00:22:09,593 --> 00:22:13,684
GOT locations, and gadgets may require a binary-base

485
00:22:13,684 --> 00:22:17,298
leak. The canary explains why a blind overflow

486
00:22:17,298 --> 00:22:18,725
can die before `ret`.

487
00:22:18,725 --> 00:22:22,055
RELRO explains why reading a GOT entry may

488
00:22:22,055 --> 00:22:26,050
remain useful even when overwriting it is blocked.

489
00:22:26,050 --> 00:22:28,652
Today we did not begin with five floating

490
00:22:28,652 --> 00:22:31,178
shields and assign them magical powers.

491
00:22:31,178 --> 00:22:33,934
We followed real source into real binaries,

492
00:22:33,934 --> 00:22:36,689
inspected their headers and instructions,

493
00:22:36,689 --> 00:22:38,832
ran them, read their live mappings,

494
00:22:38,832 --> 00:22:41,818
and then built the diagrams from that evidence.

495
00:22:41,818 --> 00:22:44,726
The result is a defender’s map of exploitation:

496
00:22:44,726 --> 00:22:47,329
executable permission, stack integrity,

497
00:22:47,329 --> 00:22:49,931
randomized placement, relocatable code,

498
00:22:49,931 --> 00:22:51,844
and hardened relocation data.

499
00:22:51,844 --> 00:22:54,217
Next time, we ask what happens when the

500
00:22:54,217 --> 00:22:55,595
program leaks the map.
